#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536184 :question/problem "Evaluate the line integral of the function $f(x,y)=9 y$ along the curve parametrized by $r(t)=\\langle t^{2},t\\rangle$ for $t\\in [0,2 \\sqrt{3}].$" :question/worked-solution "To calculate the line integral, we will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_C f\\,\\text{d}s=\\int_a^b f(r(t))\\,\\|r'(t)\\|\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $r(t)=(t^{2})\\,i+(t)\\,j$, then along the curve we have $x=t^{2}$, $y=t$.\n\nComputing $r'(t)$ and $\\|r'(t)\\|,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r'(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t^{2})\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)\\,j \\\\  & =(2 t)\\,i+(1)\\,j \\\\ \\|r'(t)\\| & =\\sqrt{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)^2+\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)^2} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(2 t)^2+(1)^2} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4 t^{2} + 1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=9 y$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =(9)(t) \\\\  & =9 t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_C f\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{2 \\sqrt{3}}f(r(t))\\,\\|r'(t)\\|\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2 \\sqrt{3}}(9 t)(\\sqrt{4 t^{2} + 1})\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this using the substitution $u=4 t^{2} + 1$, $\\text{d}u=(8 t)\\,\\text{d}t$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{2 \\sqrt{3}}9 t \\sqrt{4 t^{2} + 1}\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\int }_{1}^{49}\\frac{9}{8}\\cdot u^{1/2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{8}\\cdot\\frac{2}{3}u^{3/2}\\big|_{1}^{49} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}[(49)^{3/2}-(1)^{3/2}] \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}[(343)-(1)] \\\\  & =\\left(\\frac{1029}{4}\\right)-\\left(\\frac{3}{4}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{513}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536185 :question/problem "Evaluate the line integral of the function $f(x,y)=3 y$ along the curve parametrized by $r(t)=\\langle t^{2},t\\rangle$ for $t\\in [0,\\sqrt{2}].$" :question/worked-solution "To calculate the line integral, we will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_C f\\,\\text{d}s=\\int_a^b f(r(t))\\,\\|r'(t)\\|\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $r(t)=(t^{2})\\,i+(t)\\,j$, then along the curve we have $x=t^{2}$, $y=t$.\n\nComputing $r'(t)$ and $\\|r'(t)\\|,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r'(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t^{2})\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)\\,j \\\\  & =(2 t)\\,i+(1)\\,j \\\\ \\|r'(t)\\| & =\\sqrt{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)^2+\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)^2} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(2 t)^2+(1)^2} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4 t^{2} + 1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=3 y$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =(3)(t) \\\\  & =3 t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_C f\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}f(r(t))\\,\\|r'(t)\\|\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}(3 t)(\\sqrt{4 t^{2} + 1})\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this using the substitution $u=4 t^{2} + 1$, $\\text{d}u=(8 t)\\,\\text{d}t$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}3 t \\sqrt{4 t^{2} + 1}\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\int }_{1}^{9}\\frac{3}{8}\\cdot u^{1/2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{8}\\cdot\\frac{2}{3}u^{3/2}\\big|_{1}^{9} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[(9)^{3/2}-(1)^{3/2}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[(27)-(1)] \\\\  & =\\left(\\frac{27}{4}\\right)-\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{13}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536186 :question/problem "Evaluate the line integral of the function $f(x,y)=11 y$ along the curve parametrized by $r(t)=\\langle t^{2},t\\rangle$ for $t\\in [0,\\sqrt{6}].$" :question/worked-solution "To calculate the line integral, we will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_C f\\,\\text{d}s=\\int_a^b f(r(t))\\,\\|r'(t)\\|\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $r(t)=(t^{2})\\,i+(t)\\,j$, then along the curve we have $x=t^{2}$, $y=t$.\n\nComputing $r'(t)$ and $\\|r'(t)\\|,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r'(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t^{2})\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)\\,j \\\\  & =(2 t)\\,i+(1)\\,j \\\\ \\|r'(t)\\| & =\\sqrt{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)^2+\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)^2} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(2 t)^2+(1)^2} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4 t^{2} + 1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=11 y$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =(11)(t) \\\\  & =11 t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_C f\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{6}}f(r(t))\\,\\|r'(t)\\|\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{6}}(11 t)(\\sqrt{4 t^{2} + 1})\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this using the substitution $u=4 t^{2} + 1$, $\\text{d}u=(8 t)\\,\\text{d}t$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{6}}11 t \\sqrt{4 t^{2} + 1}\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\int }_{1}^{25}\\frac{11}{8}\\cdot u^{1/2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{11}{8}\\cdot\\frac{2}{3}u^{3/2}\\big|_{1}^{25} \\\\  & =\\frac{11}{12}[(25)^{3/2}-(1)^{3/2}] \\\\  & =\\frac{11}{12}[(125)-(1)] \\\\  & =\\left(\\frac{1375}{12}\\right)-\\left(\\frac{11}{12}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{341}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536187 :question/problem "Evaluate the line integral of the function $f(x,y,z)=\\frac{3 y + 3 z}{x}$ along the curve parametrized by $r(t)=\\langle t,t^{2},\\frac{3 t^{2}}{2}\\rangle$ for $t\\in [1,\\sqrt{2}].$" :question/worked-solution "To calculate the line integral, we will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_C f\\,\\text{d}s=\\int_a^b f(r(t))\\,\\|r'(t)\\|\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $r(t)=(t)\\,i+(t^{2})\\,j+\\left(\\frac{3 t^{2}}{2}\\right)\\,k$, then along the curve we have $x=t$, $y=t^{2}$, $z=\\frac{3 t^{2}}{2}$.\n\nComputing $r'(t)$ and $\\|r'(t)\\|,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r'(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}\\,k \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t^{2})\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\left(\\frac{3 t^{2}}{2}\\right)\\,k \\\\  & =(1)\\,i+(2 t)\\,j+(3 t)\\,k \\\\ \\|r'(t)\\| & =\\sqrt{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)^2+\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)^2+\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}\\right)^2} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(1)^2+(2 t)^2+(3 t)^2} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{13 t^{2} + 1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y,z)=\\frac{3 y + 3 z}{x}$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =\\frac{(3)[(t^{2})+\\left(\\frac{3 t^{2}}{2}\\right)]}{t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\left(\\frac{15}{2}\\right)t^2}{(1)t} \\\\  & =\\frac{15 t}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_C f\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\sqrt{2}}f(r(t))\\,\\|r'(t)\\|\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\sqrt{2}}\\left(\\frac{15 t}{2}\\right)(\\sqrt{13 t^{2} + 1})\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this using the substitution $u=13 t^{2} + 1$, $\\text{d}u=(26 t)\\,\\text{d}t$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{\\sqrt{2}}\\frac{15 t \\sqrt{13 t^{2} + 1}}{2}\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\int }_{14}^{27}\\sqrt{u}\\cdot \\frac{15}{52}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{15}{52}{\\int }_{14}^{27}u^{1/2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{15}{52}\\cdot\\frac{2}{3}u^{3/2}\\big|_{14}^{27} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{26}[(27)^{3/2}-(14)^{3/2}] \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{26}[(81 \\sqrt{3})-(14 \\sqrt{14})] \\\\  & =\\left(\\frac{405 \\sqrt{3}}{26}\\right)-\\left(\\frac{35 \\sqrt{14}}{13}\\right) \\\\  & =- \\frac{35 \\sqrt{14}}{13} + \\frac{405 \\sqrt{3}}{26}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536188 :question/problem "Evaluate the line integral of the function $f(x,y,z)=\\frac{4 y + 4 z}{x}$ along the curve parametrized by $r(t)=\\langle 2 t,\\frac{3 t^{2}}{2},t^{2}\\rangle$ for $t\\in [\\sqrt{2},2].$" :question/worked-solution "To calculate the line integral, we will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_C f\\,\\text{d}s=\\int_a^b f(r(t))\\,\\|r'(t)\\|\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $r(t)=(2 t)\\,i+\\left(\\frac{3 t^{2}}{2}\\right)\\,j+(t^{2})\\,k$, then along the curve we have $x=2 t$, $y=\\frac{3 t^{2}}{2}$, $z=t^{2}$.\n\nComputing $r'(t)$ and $\\|r'(t)\\|,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r'(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}\\,k \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2 t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\left(\\frac{3 t^{2}}{2}\\right)\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t^{2})\\,k \\\\  & =(2)\\,i+(3 t)\\,j+(2 t)\\,k \\\\ \\|r'(t)\\| & =\\sqrt{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)^2+\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)^2+\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}\\right)^2} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(2)^2+(3 t)^2+(2 t)^2} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{13 t^{2} + 4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y,z)=\\frac{4 y + 4 z}{x}$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =\\frac{(4)[\\left(\\frac{3 t^{2}}{2}\\right)+(t^{2})]}{2 t} \\\\  & =\\frac{(10)t^2}{(2)t} \\\\  & =5 t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_C f\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{\\sqrt{2}}^{2}f(r(t))\\,\\|r'(t)\\|\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\sqrt{2}}^{2}(5 t)(\\sqrt{13 t^{2} + 4})\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this using the substitution $u=13 t^{2} + 4$, $\\text{d}u=(26 t)\\,\\text{d}t$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{\\sqrt{2}}^{2}5 t \\sqrt{13 t^{2} + 4}\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\int }_{30}^{56}\\sqrt{u}\\cdot \\frac{5}{26}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{26}{\\int }_{30}^{56}u^{1/2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{26}\\cdot\\frac{2}{3}u^{3/2}\\big|_{30}^{56} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{39}[(56)^{3/2}-(30)^{3/2}] \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{39}[(112 \\sqrt{14})-(30 \\sqrt{30})] \\\\  & =\\left(\\frac{560 \\sqrt{14}}{39}\\right)-\\left(\\frac{50 \\sqrt{30}}{13}\\right) \\\\  & =- \\frac{50 \\sqrt{30}}{13} + \\frac{560 \\sqrt{14}}{39}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536195 :question/problem "Evaluate the line integral of the function $f(x,y,z)=\\frac{2 y + 2 z}{x}$ along the curve parametrized by $r(t)=\\langle 2 t,t^{2},\\frac{3 t^{2}}{2}\\rangle$ for $t\\in [\\sqrt{2},\\sqrt{3}].$" :question/worked-solution "To calculate the line integral, we will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_C f\\,\\text{d}s=\\int_a^b f(r(t))\\,\\|r'(t)\\|\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $r(t)=(2 t)\\,i+(t^{2})\\,j+\\left(\\frac{3 t^{2}}{2}\\right)\\,k$, then along the curve we have $x=2 t$, $y=t^{2}$, $z=\\frac{3 t^{2}}{2}$.\n\nComputing $r'(t)$ and $\\|r'(t)\\|,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r'(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}\\,k \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2 t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t^{2})\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\left(\\frac{3 t^{2}}{2}\\right)\\,k \\\\  & =(2)\\,i+(2 t)\\,j+(3 t)\\,k \\\\ \\|r'(t)\\| & =\\sqrt{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)^2+\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)^2+\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}\\right)^2} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(2)^2+(2 t)^2+(3 t)^2} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{13 t^{2} + 4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y,z)=\\frac{2 y + 2 z}{x}$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =\\frac{(2)[(t^{2})+\\left(\\frac{3 t^{2}}{2}\\right)]}{2 t} \\\\  & =\\frac{(5)t^2}{(2)t} \\\\  & =\\frac{5 t}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_C f\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{\\sqrt{2}}^{\\sqrt{3}}f(r(t))\\,\\|r'(t)\\|\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\sqrt{2}}^{\\sqrt{3}}\\left(\\frac{5 t}{2}\\right)(\\sqrt{13 t^{2} + 4})\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this using the substitution $u=13 t^{2} + 4$, $\\text{d}u=(26 t)\\,\\text{d}t$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{\\sqrt{2}}^{\\sqrt{3}}\\frac{5 t \\sqrt{13 t^{2} + 4}}{2}\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\int }_{30}^{43}\\sqrt{u}\\cdot \\frac{5}{52}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{52}{\\int }_{30}^{43}u^{1/2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{52}\\cdot\\frac{2}{3}u^{3/2}\\big|_{30}^{43} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{78}[(43)^{3/2}-(30)^{3/2}] \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{78}[(43 \\sqrt{43})-(30 \\sqrt{30})] \\\\  & =\\left(\\frac{215 \\sqrt{43}}{78}\\right)-\\left(\\frac{25 \\sqrt{30}}{13}\\right) \\\\  & =- \\frac{25 \\sqrt{30}}{13} + \\frac{215 \\sqrt{43}}{78}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536197 :question/problem "Evaluate the line integral of the function $f(x,y,z)=\\frac{6 y + 6 z}{x}$ along the curve parametrized by $r(t)=\\langle 3 t,t^{2},t^{2}\\rangle$ for $t\\in [\\sqrt{2},2].$" :question/worked-solution "To calculate the line integral, we will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_C f\\,\\text{d}s=\\int_a^b f(r(t))\\,\\|r'(t)\\|\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $r(t)=(3 t)\\,i+(t^{2})\\,j+(t^{2})\\,k$, then along the curve we have $x=3 t$, $y=t^{2}$, $z=t^{2}$.\n\nComputing $r'(t)$ and $\\|r'(t)\\|,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r'(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}\\,k \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3 t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t^{2})\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t^{2})\\,k \\\\  & =(3)\\,i+(2 t)\\,j+(2 t)\\,k \\\\ \\|r'(t)\\| & =\\sqrt{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)^2+\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)^2+\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}\\right)^2} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(3)^2+(2 t)^2+(2 t)^2} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{8 t^{2} + 9}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y,z)=\\frac{6 y + 6 z}{x}$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =\\frac{(6)[(t^{2})+(t^{2})]}{3 t} \\\\  & =\\frac{(12)t^2}{(3)t} \\\\  & =4 t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_C f\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{\\sqrt{2}}^{2}f(r(t))\\,\\|r'(t)\\|\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\sqrt{2}}^{2}(4 t)(\\sqrt{8 t^{2} + 9})\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this using the substitution $u=8 t^{2} + 9$, $\\text{d}u=(16 t)\\,\\text{d}t$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{\\sqrt{2}}^{2}4 t \\sqrt{8 t^{2} + 9}\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\int }_{25}^{41}\\sqrt{u}\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{25}^{41}u^{1/2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot\\frac{2}{3}u^{3/2}\\big|_{25}^{41} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}[(41)^{3/2}-(25)^{3/2}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}[(41 \\sqrt{41})-(125)] \\\\  & =\\left(\\frac{41 \\sqrt{41}}{6}\\right)-\\left(\\frac{125}{6}\\right) \\\\  & =- \\frac{125}{6} + \\frac{41 \\sqrt{41}}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536219 :question/problem "Evaluate the line integral of the function $f(x,y)=10 y$ along the curve parametrized by $r(t)=\\langle t^{2},t\\rangle$ for $t\\in [0,2 \\sqrt{3}].$" :question/worked-solution "To calculate the line integral, we will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_C f\\,\\text{d}s=\\int_a^b f(r(t))\\,\\|r'(t)\\|\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $r(t)=(t^{2})\\,i+(t)\\,j$, then along the curve we have $x=t^{2}$, $y=t$.\n\nComputing $r'(t)$ and $\\|r'(t)\\|,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r'(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t^{2})\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)\\,j \\\\  & =(2 t)\\,i+(1)\\,j \\\\ \\|r'(t)\\| & =\\sqrt{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)^2+\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)^2} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(2 t)^2+(1)^2} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4 t^{2} + 1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=10 y$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =(10)(t) \\\\  & =10 t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_C f\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{2 \\sqrt{3}}f(r(t))\\,\\|r'(t)\\|\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2 \\sqrt{3}}(10 t)(\\sqrt{4 t^{2} + 1})\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this using the substitution $u=4 t^{2} + 1$, $\\text{d}u=(8 t)\\,\\text{d}t$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{2 \\sqrt{3}}10 t \\sqrt{4 t^{2} + 1}\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\int }_{1}^{49}\\frac{5}{4}\\cdot u^{1/2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{4}\\cdot\\frac{2}{3}u^{3/2}\\big|_{1}^{49} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{6}[(49)^{3/2}-(1)^{3/2}] \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{6}[(343)-(1)] \\\\  & =\\left(\\frac{1715}{6}\\right)-\\left(\\frac{5}{6}\\right) \\\\  & =285\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536220 :question/problem "Evaluate the line integral of the function $f(x,y)=3 \\cos{\\left(x \\right)}$ along the curve parametrized by $r(t)=\\langle 2 t^{2},- 4 t^{2}\\rangle$ for $t\\in [0,\\frac{\\sqrt{3} \\sqrt{\\pi}}{6}].$" :question/worked-solution "To calculate the line integral, we will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_C f\\,\\text{d}s=\\int_a^b f(r(t))\\,\\|r'(t)\\|\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $r(t)=(2 t^{2})\\,i+(- 4 t^{2})\\,j$, then along the curve we have $x=2 t^{2}$, $y=- 4 t^{2}$.\n\nComputing $r'(t)$ and $\\|r'(t)\\|,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r'(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2 t^{2})\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(- 4 t^{2})\\,j \\\\  & =(4 t)\\,i+(- 8 t)\\,j \\\\ \\|r'(t)\\| & =\\sqrt{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)^2+\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)^2} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(4 t)^2+(- 8 t)^2} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{80 t^{2}} \\\\  & =4 \\sqrt{5}|t| \\\\  & =4 \\sqrt{5} t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $|t|=t$ because $t\\geq0$ along the path of $C.$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=3 \\cos{\\left(x \\right)}$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =3 \\cos{\\left(2 t^{2} \\right)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_C f\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\frac{\\sqrt{3} \\sqrt{\\pi}}{6}}f(r(t))\\,\\|r'(t)\\|\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\frac{\\sqrt{3} \\sqrt{\\pi}}{6}}(3 \\cos{\\left(2 t^{2} \\right)})(4 \\sqrt{5} t)\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this using the substitution $u=2 t^{2}$, $\\text{d}u=(4 t)\\,\\text{d}t$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\frac{\\sqrt{3} \\sqrt{\\pi}}{6}}12 \\sqrt{5} t \\cos{\\left(2 t^{2} \\right)}\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{6}}3 \\sqrt{5}\\cdot \\cos u\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =3 \\sqrt{5}\\sin u\\big|_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{6}} \\\\  & =3 \\sqrt{5}[\\sin\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{6}\\right)-\\sin(0)] \\\\  & =3 \\sqrt{5}[\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)-(0)] \\\\  & =\\left(\\frac{3 \\sqrt{5}}{2}\\right)-(0) \\\\  & =\\frac{3 \\sqrt{5}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536221 :question/problem "Evaluate the line integral of the function $f(x,y)=4 y$ along the curve parametrized by $r(t)=\\langle t^{2},t\\rangle$ for $t\\in [0,\\sqrt{6}].$" :question/worked-solution "To calculate the line integral, we will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_C f\\,\\text{d}s=\\int_a^b f(r(t))\\,\\|r'(t)\\|\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $r(t)=(t^{2})\\,i+(t)\\,j$, then along the curve we have $x=t^{2}$, $y=t$.\n\nComputing $r'(t)$ and $\\|r'(t)\\|,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r'(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t^{2})\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)\\,j \\\\  & =(2 t)\\,i+(1)\\,j \\\\ \\|r'(t)\\| & =\\sqrt{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)^2+\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)^2} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(2 t)^2+(1)^2} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4 t^{2} + 1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=4 y$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =(4)(t) \\\\  & =4 t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_C f\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{6}}f(r(t))\\,\\|r'(t)\\|\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{6}}(4 t)(\\sqrt{4 t^{2} + 1})\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this using the substitution $u=4 t^{2} + 1$, $\\text{d}u=(8 t)\\,\\text{d}t$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{6}}4 t \\sqrt{4 t^{2} + 1}\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\int }_{1}^{25}\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot u^{1/2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot\\frac{2}{3}u^{3/2}\\big|_{1}^{25} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}[(25)^{3/2}-(1)^{3/2}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}[(125)-(1)] \\\\  & =\\left(\\frac{125}{3}\\right)-\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{124}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536251 :question/problem "Which of the following gives the length of the curve defined by the parametric equations\n$$\n\\displaystyle x=2 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)},\\,y=4 t + \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\n$$\nfrom $t=-1$ to $t=1$?" :question/worked-solution "To compute the arc length $L$ of a parametrically defined curve, we use the formula\n\n$$\nL=\\int_{t_1}^{t_2}\\sqrt{\\left(\\frac{\\mathrm{d}x}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\right)^2+\\left(\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\right)^2}\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}x}{\\mathrm{d}t}=\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\left(2 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)=- 2 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}t}=\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\left(4 t + \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)=4 - \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}.\n$$\n\nWe substitute these derivatives in the formula for arc length, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L&=\\int_{t_1}^{t_2}\\sqrt{\\left(\\frac{\\mathrm{d}x}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\right)^2+\\left(\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\right)^2}\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\ &=\\int_{-1}^{1}\\sqrt{\\left(- 2 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)^2+\\left(4 - \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)^2}\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\ &=\\int_{-1}^{1}\\sqrt{4\\sin^2(t)+16-8\\sin(t)+\\sin^2(t)}\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\ &=\\int_{-1}^{1}\\sqrt{5 \\sin^{2}{\\left(t \\right)} - 8 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} + 16}\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536259 :question/problem "Which of the following gives the length of the curve defined by the parametric equations\n$$\n\\displaystyle x=2 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)},\\,y=6 t + \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\n$$\nfrom $t=-1$ to $t=1$?" :question/worked-solution "To compute the arc length $L$ of a parametrically defined curve, we use the formula\n\n$$\nL=\\int_{t_1}^{t_2}\\sqrt{\\left(\\frac{\\mathrm{d}x}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\right)^2+\\left(\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\right)^2}\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}x}{\\mathrm{d}t}=\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\left(2 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)=- 2 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}t}=\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\left(6 t + \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)=6 - \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}.\n$$\n\nWe substitute these derivatives in the formula for arc length, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L&=\\int_{t_1}^{t_2}\\sqrt{\\left(\\frac{\\mathrm{d}x}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\right)^2+\\left(\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\right)^2}\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\ &=\\int_{-1}^{1}\\sqrt{\\left(- 2 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)^2+\\left(6 - \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)^2}\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\ &=\\int_{-1}^{1}\\sqrt{4\\sin^2(t)+36-12\\sin(t)+\\sin^2(t)}\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\ &=\\int_{-1}^{1}\\sqrt{5 \\sin^{2}{\\left(t \\right)} - 12 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} + 36}\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536262 :question/problem "Which of the following gives the length of the curve defined by the parametric equations\n$$\n\\displaystyle x=2 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)},\\,y=5 t + \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\n$$\nfrom $t=-1$ to $t=1$?" :question/worked-solution "To compute the arc length $L$ of a parametrically defined curve, we use the formula\n\n$$\nL=\\int_{t_1}^{t_2}\\sqrt{\\left(\\frac{\\mathrm{d}x}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\right)^2+\\left(\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\right)^2}\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}x}{\\mathrm{d}t}=\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\left(2 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)=- 2 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}t}=\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\left(5 t + \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)=5 - \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}.\n$$\n\nWe substitute these derivatives in the formula for arc length, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L&=\\int_{t_1}^{t_2}\\sqrt{\\left(\\frac{\\mathrm{d}x}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\right)^2+\\left(\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\right)^2}\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\ &=\\int_{-1}^{1}\\sqrt{\\left(- 2 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)^2+\\left(5 - \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)^2}\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\ &=\\int_{-1}^{1}\\sqrt{4\\sin^2(t)+25-10\\sin(t)+\\sin^2(t)}\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\ &=\\int_{-1}^{1}\\sqrt{5 \\sin^{2}{\\left(t \\right)} - 10 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} + 25}\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536821 :question/problem "Find the value of ${a}_{5},$ the fifth nonzero term of the alternating series $$\n\\displaystyle S={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}.\n$$\n\n${a}_{5}\\,=\\,$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that the terms of our series $$\n\\displaystyle S={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\n$$ are the coefficients of the Maclaurin series for $g(x).$\n\nPreviously, we found the fourth-degree Taylor polynomial of $f(x):$\n\n$$\nf(x)\\approx {P}_{4}(x)=2-\\frac{1}{2}x+\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{2}-\\frac{1}{6}{x}^{3}+{\\color{blue}\\frac{1}{8}{x}^{4}}\n$$\n\nTo find the ${x}^{5}$ term of the Maclaurin series of $g(x),$ we simply need to integrate the ${x}^{4}$ term of ${P}_{4}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{x}\\frac{1}{8}{t}^{4}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{8}{\\int }_{0}^{x}{t}^{4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{40}{t}^{5}\\,{|}_{0}^{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{40}{x}^{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, ${a}_{5}$ is the coefficient of $\\frac{1}{40}{x}^{5},$ that is, ${a}_{5}=\\frac{1}{40}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536832 :question/problem "![](images/50/5034bb902aadd151cc1a28764e054dbdd6a694ad653f50557e5fc208c8ec2342.png)\n\nFind the arc length of the polar curve $r=1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ for $0\\le \\theta \\le \\pi $ (shown in blue in the diagram above).\n\n*Hint: You may wish to use the following identity: $$\n\\displaystyle \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right)=\\sqrt{\\frac{1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{2}}.\n$$*" :question/worked-solution "The arc length $L$ of a polar curve $r=r(\\theta )$ is given by\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r=1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, the arc length is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{(1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+{(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{1+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{1+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +1}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{2+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we recall the identity for $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right):$\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right)=\\sqrt{\\frac{1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{2}}\n$$\n\nUsing $x=\\theta $ in the above identity, the integral can be rewritten as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & =\\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{2{cos}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\theta }{2}\\right)}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\sqrt{2}\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\theta }{2}\\right)\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={2\\cdot 2(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\theta }{2}\\right))|}_{0}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =4(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡0) \\\\  & =4(1-0) \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536856 :question/problem "Find the value of ${f}^{′}(0).$\n\n${f}^{′}(0)\\,=\\,$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Recall that ${f}^{′}(0)$ is just the slope of the tangent line to the graph of $y=f(x)$ at the point where $x=0.$\n\nNotice that our tangent line passes through the points $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(4,0)$ and $({x}_{2},{y}_{2})=(0,2).$ Therefore, we can compute its slope as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}} & =\\frac{2-0}{0-4} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{-4} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${f}^{′}(0)=-\\frac{1}{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536874 :question/problem "Let $\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z)=yz\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+xz\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+xy\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}$ and let $C$ be a path in ${\\mathbb{R}}^{3}.$ Which of the following is equivalent to $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r?\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Given a piecewise-smooth path $C$ and a vector field \n\n$$\nF(x,y,z)=P(x,y,z)\\,i+Q(x,y,z)\\,j+R(x,y,z)\\,k\n$$\n\ndefined on $C,$ we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={\\int }_{C}P(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}x+Q(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}y+R(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}z.\n$$\n\nTherefore, in our case, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{C}yz\\,\\text{d}x+xz\\,\\text{d}y+xy\\,\\text{d}z.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536983 :question/problem "If the solid region $R$ is given by \n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,(x-2)^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 4,y\\ge 0,\\,0\\le z\\le 1\\},\n$$\n\nthen express the triple integral\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}x^{2} + y^{2}\\,\\text{d}V\n$$\n\nas an equivalent integral in cylindrical polar coordinates." :question/worked-solution "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,dV={∭}_{Δ}\\,f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,z)\\,r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta .\n$$\n\nIf $(r,\\theta ,z)$ are the cylindrical polar coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the formulas\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,z=z.\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the projection of $R$ onto the $xy$-plane:\n\n![](images/d2/d2b3344b7b910f5685d4fd81b75a600608af6b008778ff608cb084edb5a92da9.png)\n\nThe region $R$ is one-half of a right vertical cylinder of radius $2$ whose axis passes through the point $(2,0)$ in the $xy$-plane, and is\n\n- bounded below by $z=0,$ and\n\n- bounded above by $z=1.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write the $(x-2)^{2}+{y}^{2}=4$ in polar coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-2)^{2}+{y}^{2} & =4 \\\\ (r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta-2)^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta)^{2} & =4 \\\\ {r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -4r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta+4+{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta & =4 \\\\ {r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )-4r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta & =0 \\\\ {r}^{2}-4r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta & =0 \\\\ r(r-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $r$ cannot be identical to zero on the entire semicircle, we must have $r=4 \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}.$\n\nAs a result, in cylindrical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta ,z)\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 4 \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)},\\,0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi}{2},\\,0\\le z\\le 1\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}x^{2} + y^{2}\\,\\text{d}V & ={∭}_{Δ}((r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta)^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta)^{2})\\cdot r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta \\\\  & ={∭}_{Δ}{r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\cdot r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}{\\int }_{0}^{4 \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}{\\int }_{0}^{1}r^{3}\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537003 :question/problem "If the solid region $R$ is given by \n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+(y+2)^{2}\\le 4,\\,0\\le z\\le x^{2} + y^{2} + 5\\},\n$$\n\nthen express the triple integral\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}x y\\,\\text{d}V\n$$\n\nas an equivalent integral in cylindrical polar coordinates." :question/worked-solution "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,dV={∭}_{Δ}\\,f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,z)\\,r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta .\n$$\n\nIf $(r,\\theta ,z)$ are the cylindrical polar coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the formulas\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,z=z.\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the projection of $R$ onto the $xy$-plane:\n\n![](images/ce/cee08173667c9577f4c7aab8cac5386434f2e1ecab8836150ebe8066d1cbaa68.png)\n\nThe region $R$ is a right vertical cylinder of radius $2$ whose axis passes through the point $(0,-2)$ in the $xy$-plane, and is\n\n- bounded below by $z=0,$ and\n\n- bounded above by $z=x^{2} + y^{2} + 5=r^{2} + 5.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's write the equation ${x}^{2}+(y+2)^{2}=4$ in polar coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+(y+2)^{2} & =4 \\\\ (r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta)^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta+2)^{2} & =4 \\\\ {r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +4r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta+4 & =4 \\\\ {r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )+4r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta & =0 \\\\ {r}^{2}+4r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta & =0 \\\\ r(r+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $r$ cannot be identical to zero on the entire circle, we must have $r=- 4 \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}.$\n\nAs a result, in cylindrical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta ,z)\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le - 4 \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)},\\,\\pi\\le \\theta \\le 2 \\pi,\\,0\\le z\\le r^{2} + 5\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}x y\\,\\text{d}V & ={∭}_{Δ}(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta)(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta)\\cdot r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi}^{2 \\pi}{\\int }_{0}^{- 4 \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}{\\int }_{0}^{r^{2} + 5}r^{3} \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)} \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537006 :question/problem "If the solid region $R$ is given by \n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+(y-4)^{2}\\le 16,x\\le 0,\\,-5\\le z\\le \\sqrt{- x^{2} - y^{2} + 81}\\},\n$$\n\nthen express the triple integral\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}2 x y\\,\\text{d}V\n$$\n\nas an equivalent integral in cylindrical polar coordinates." :question/worked-solution "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,dV={∭}_{Δ}\\,f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,z)\\,r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta .\n$$\n\nIf $(r,\\theta ,z)$ are the cylindrical polar coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the formulas\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,z=z.\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the projection of $R$ onto the $xy$-plane:\n\n![](images/57/5764f328a614ddaa96abd28a4568c4cd7dfcf0988033d3122bdce0e1e52b7bc5.png)\n\nThe region $R$ is one-half of a right vertical cylinder of radius $4$ whose axis passes through the point $(0,4)$ in the $xy$-plane, and is\n\n- bounded below by $z=-5,$ and\n\n- bounded above by $z=\\sqrt{- x^{2} - y^{2} + 81}=\\sqrt{81 - r^{2}}.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write the ${x}^{2}+(y-4)^{2}=16$ in polar coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+(y-4)^{2} & =16 \\\\ (r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta)^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta-4)^{2} & =16 \\\\ {r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta -8r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta+16 & =16 \\\\ {r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )-8r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta & =0 \\\\ {r}^{2}-8r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta & =0 \\\\ r(r-8\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $r$ cannot be identical to zero on the entire semicircle, we must have $r=8 \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}.$\n\nAs a result, in cylindrical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta ,z)\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 8 \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)},\\,\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\le \\theta \\le \\pi,\\,-5\\le z\\le \\sqrt{81 - r^{2}}\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}2 x y\\,\\text{d}V & ={∭}_{Δ}2(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta)(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta)\\cdot r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{\\pi}{\\int }_{0}^{8 \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}{\\int }_{-5}^{\\sqrt{81 - r^{2}}}2 r^{3} \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)} \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537019 :question/problem "If the solid region $R$ is given by \n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,(x-2)^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 4,y\\ge 0,\\,4\\le z\\le 7\\},\n$$\n\nthen express the triple integral\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}x^{2} + y^{2}\\,\\text{d}V\n$$\n\nas an equivalent integral in cylindrical polar coordinates." :question/worked-solution "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,dV={∭}_{Δ}\\,f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,z)\\,r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta .\n$$\n\nIf $(r,\\theta ,z)$ are the cylindrical polar coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the formulas\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,z=z.\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the projection of $R$ onto the $xy$-plane:\n\n![](images/d2/d2b3344b7b910f5685d4fd81b75a600608af6b008778ff608cb084edb5a92da9.png)\n\nThe region $R$ is one-half of a right vertical cylinder of radius $2$ whose axis passes through the point $(2,0)$ in the $xy$-plane, and is\n\n- bounded below by $z=4,$ and\n\n- bounded above by $z=7.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write the $(x-2)^{2}+{y}^{2}=4$ in polar coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-2)^{2}+{y}^{2} & =4 \\\\ (r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta-2)^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta)^{2} & =4 \\\\ {r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -4r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta+4+{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta & =4 \\\\ {r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )-4r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta & =0 \\\\ {r}^{2}-4r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta & =0 \\\\ r(r-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $r$ cannot be identical to zero on the entire semicircle, we must have $r=4 \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}.$\n\nAs a result, in cylindrical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta ,z)\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 4 \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)},\\,0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi}{2},\\,4\\le z\\le 7\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}x^{2} + y^{2}\\,\\text{d}V & ={∭}_{Δ}((r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta)^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta)^{2})\\cdot r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta \\\\  & ={∭}_{Δ}{r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\cdot r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}{\\int }_{0}^{4 \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}{\\int }_{4}^{7}r^{3}\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537025 :question/problem "If the solid region $R$ is given by \n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,(x-4)^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 16,y\\ge 0,\\,4\\le z\\le 7\\},\n$$\n\nthen express the triple integral\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}x^{2} + y^{2}\\,\\text{d}V\n$$\n\nas an equivalent integral in cylindrical polar coordinates." :question/worked-solution "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,dV={∭}_{Δ}\\,f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,z)\\,r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta .\n$$\n\nIf $(r,\\theta ,z)$ are the cylindrical polar coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the formulas\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,z=z.\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the projection of $R$ onto the $xy$-plane:\n\n![](images/80/80a919b28ae7ed559b06b34bd8512859794a7d6550970b56347e2deb653bf76d.png)\n\nThe region $R$ is one-half of a right vertical cylinder of radius $4$ whose axis passes through the point $(4,0)$ in the $xy$-plane, and is\n\n- bounded below by $z=4,$ and\n\n- bounded above by $z=7.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write the $(x-4)^{2}+{y}^{2}=16$ in polar coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-4)^{2}+{y}^{2} & =16 \\\\ (r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta-4)^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta)^{2} & =16 \\\\ {r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -8r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta+16+{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta & =16 \\\\ {r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )-8r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta & =0 \\\\ {r}^{2}-8r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta & =0 \\\\ r(r-8\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $r$ cannot be identical to zero on the entire semicircle, we must have $r=8 \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}.$\n\nAs a result, in cylindrical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta ,z)\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 8 \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)},\\,0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi}{2},\\,4\\le z\\le 7\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}x^{2} + y^{2}\\,\\text{d}V & ={∭}_{Δ}((r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta)^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta)^{2})\\cdot r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta \\\\  & ={∭}_{Δ}{r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\cdot r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}{\\int }_{0}^{8 \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}{\\int }_{4}^{7}r^{3}\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537027 :question/problem "If the solid region $R$ is given by \n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,(x+2)^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 4,\\,-1\\le z\\le x + 5\\},\n$$\n\nthen express the triple integral\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x^{2} + y^{2} + 2}}\\,\\text{d}V\n$$\n\nas an equivalent integral in cylindrical polar coordinates." :question/worked-solution "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,dV={∭}_{Δ}\\,f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,z)\\,r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta .\n$$\n\nIf $(r,\\theta ,z)$ are the cylindrical polar coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the formulas\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,z=z.\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the projection of $R$ onto the $xy$-plane:\n\n![](images/f8/f830c1469527c615d213ffcd11e472083c77d7e5fbc8c1577a85244fb5739fc4.png)\n\nThe region $R$ is a right vertical cylinder of radius $2$ whose axis passes through the point $(-2,0)$ in the $xy$-plane, and is\n\n- bounded below by $z=-1,$ and\n\n- bounded above by $z=x + 5=r \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)} + 5.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's write the equation $(x+2)^{2}+{y}^{2}=4$ in polar coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+2)^{2}+{y}^{2} & =4 \\\\ (r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta+2)^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta)^{2} & =4 \\\\ {r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +4r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta+4+{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta & =4 \\\\ {r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )+4r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta & =0 \\\\ {r}^{2}+4r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta & =0 \\\\ r(r+4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $r$ cannot be identical to zero on the entire circle, we must have $r=- 4 \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}.$\n\nAs a result, in cylindrical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta ,z)\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le - 4 \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)},\\,\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{3 \\pi}{2},\\,-1\\le z\\le r \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)} + 5\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x^{2} + y^{2} + 2}}\\,\\text{d}V & ={∭}_{Δ}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta)^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta)^{2}+2}}\\cdot r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta \\\\  & ={∭}_{Δ}\\frac{r}{\\sqrt{{r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )+2}}\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{\\frac{3 \\pi}{2}}{\\int }_{0}^{- 4 \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}{\\int }_{-1}^{r \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)} + 5}\\frac{r}{\\sqrt{r^{2} + 2}}\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537040 :question/problem "If the solid region $R$ is given by \n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,(x+2)^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 4,\\,-4\\le z\\le x + 1\\},\n$$\n\nthen express the triple integral\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x^{2} + y^{2} + 2}}\\,\\text{d}V\n$$\n\nas an equivalent integral in cylindrical polar coordinates." :question/worked-solution "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,dV={∭}_{Δ}\\,f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,z)\\,r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta .\n$$\n\nIf $(r,\\theta ,z)$ are the cylindrical polar coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the formulas\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,z=z.\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the projection of $R$ onto the $xy$-plane:\n\n![](images/f8/f830c1469527c615d213ffcd11e472083c77d7e5fbc8c1577a85244fb5739fc4.png)\n\nThe region $R$ is a right vertical cylinder of radius $2$ whose axis passes through the point $(-2,0)$ in the $xy$-plane, and is\n\n- bounded below by $z=-4,$ and\n\n- bounded above by $z=x + 1=r \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)} + 1.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's write the equation $(x+2)^{2}+{y}^{2}=4$ in polar coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+2)^{2}+{y}^{2} & =4 \\\\ (r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta+2)^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta)^{2} & =4 \\\\ {r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +4r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta+4+{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta & =4 \\\\ {r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )+4r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta & =0 \\\\ {r}^{2}+4r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta & =0 \\\\ r(r+4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $r$ cannot be identical to zero on the entire circle, we must have $r=- 4 \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}.$\n\nAs a result, in cylindrical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta ,z)\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le - 4 \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)},\\,\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{3 \\pi}{2},\\,-4\\le z\\le r \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)} + 1\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x^{2} + y^{2} + 2}}\\,\\text{d}V & ={∭}_{Δ}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta)^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta)^{2}+2}}\\cdot r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta \\\\  & ={∭}_{Δ}\\frac{r}{\\sqrt{{r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )+2}}\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{\\frac{3 \\pi}{2}}{\\int }_{0}^{- 4 \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}{\\int }_{-4}^{r \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)} + 1}\\frac{r}{\\sqrt{r^{2} + 2}}\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537047 :question/problem "If the solid region $R$ is given by \n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,(x+4)^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 16,\\,-6\\le z\\le x + 5\\},\n$$\n\nthen express the triple integral\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x^{2} + y^{2} + 2}}\\,\\text{d}V\n$$\n\nas an equivalent integral in cylindrical polar coordinates." :question/worked-solution "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,dV={∭}_{Δ}\\,f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,z)\\,r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta .\n$$\n\nIf $(r,\\theta ,z)$ are the cylindrical polar coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the formulas\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,z=z.\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the projection of $R$ onto the $xy$-plane:\n\n![](images/8b/8b16f7e69a4ce84b0b7c4597defd9e9f55593e50623f344f001d24518d59c483.png)\n\nThe region $R$ is a right vertical cylinder of radius $4$ whose axis passes through the point $(-4,0)$ in the $xy$-plane, and is\n\n- bounded below by $z=-6,$ and\n\n- bounded above by $z=x + 5=r \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)} + 5.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's write the equation $(x+4)^{2}+{y}^{2}=16$ in polar coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+4)^{2}+{y}^{2} & =16 \\\\ (r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta+4)^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta)^{2} & =16 \\\\ {r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +8r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta+16+{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta & =16 \\\\ {r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )+8r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta & =0 \\\\ {r}^{2}+8r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta & =0 \\\\ r(r+8\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $r$ cannot be identical to zero on the entire circle, we must have $r=- 8 \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}.$\n\nAs a result, in cylindrical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta ,z)\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le - 8 \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)},\\,\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{3 \\pi}{2},\\,-6\\le z\\le r \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)} + 5\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x^{2} + y^{2} + 2}}\\,\\text{d}V & ={∭}_{Δ}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta)^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta)^{2}+2}}\\cdot r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta \\\\  & ={∭}_{Δ}\\frac{r}{\\sqrt{{r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )+2}}\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{\\frac{3 \\pi}{2}}{\\int }_{0}^{- 8 \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}{\\int }_{-6}^{r \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)} + 5}\\frac{r}{\\sqrt{r^{2} + 2}}\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537048 :question/problem "If the solid region $R$ is given by \n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+(y-4)^{2}\\le 16,x\\ge 0,\\,-2\\le z\\le x\\},\n$$\n\nthen express the triple integral\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}\\sqrt{x^{2} + y^{2} + 1}\\,\\text{d}V\n$$\n\nas an equivalent integral in cylindrical polar coordinates." :question/worked-solution "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,dV={∭}_{Δ}\\,f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,z)\\,r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta .\n$$\n\nIf $(r,\\theta ,z)$ are the cylindrical polar coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the formulas\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,z=z.\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the projection of $R$ onto the $xy$-plane:\n\n![](images/26/262fdb09ab8f6ee5de1a31cec2d238cb633ae2d5dfa2e99d80ed58d4cb849ec5.png)\n\nThe region $R$ is one-half of a right vertical cylinder of radius $4$ whose axis passes through the point $(0,4)$ in the $xy$-plane, and is\n\n- bounded below by $z=-2,$ and\n\n- bounded above by $z=x=r \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write the ${x}^{2}+(y-4)^{2}=16$ in polar coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+(y-4)^{2} & =16 \\\\ (r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta)^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta-4)^{2} & =16 \\\\ {r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta -8r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta+16 & =16 \\\\ {r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )-8r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta & =0 \\\\ {r}^{2}-8r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta & =0 \\\\ r(r-8\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $r$ cannot be identical to zero on the entire semicircle, we must have $r=8 \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}.$\n\nAs a result, in cylindrical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta ,z)\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 8 \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)},\\,0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi}{2},\\,-2\\le z\\le r \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}\\sqrt{x^{2} + y^{2} + 1}\\,\\text{d}V & ={∭}_{Δ}\\sqrt{(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta)^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta)^{2}+1}\\cdot r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta \\\\  & ={∭}_{Δ}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )+1}\\cdot r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}{\\int }_{0}^{8 \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}{\\int }_{-2}^{r \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}r \\sqrt{r^{2} + 1}\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537052 :question/problem "If the solid region $R$ is given by \n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,(x-4)^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 16,y\\ge 0,\\,1\\le z\\le 2\\},\n$$\n\nthen express the triple integral\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}x^{2} + y^{2}\\,\\text{d}V\n$$\n\nas an equivalent integral in cylindrical polar coordinates." :question/worked-solution "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,dV={∭}_{Δ}\\,f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,z)\\,r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta .\n$$\n\nIf $(r,\\theta ,z)$ are the cylindrical polar coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the formulas\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,z=z.\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the projection of $R$ onto the $xy$-plane:\n\n![](images/80/80a919b28ae7ed559b06b34bd8512859794a7d6550970b56347e2deb653bf76d.png)\n\nThe region $R$ is one-half of a right vertical cylinder of radius $4$ whose axis passes through the point $(4,0)$ in the $xy$-plane, and is\n\n- bounded below by $z=1,$ and\n\n- bounded above by $z=2.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write the $(x-4)^{2}+{y}^{2}=16$ in polar coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-4)^{2}+{y}^{2} & =16 \\\\ (r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta-4)^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta)^{2} & =16 \\\\ {r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -8r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta+16+{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta & =16 \\\\ {r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )-8r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta & =0 \\\\ {r}^{2}-8r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta & =0 \\\\ r(r-8\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $r$ cannot be identical to zero on the entire semicircle, we must have $r=8 \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}.$\n\nAs a result, in cylindrical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta ,z)\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 8 \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)},\\,0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi}{2},\\,1\\le z\\le 2\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}x^{2} + y^{2}\\,\\text{d}V & ={∭}_{Δ}((r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta)^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta)^{2})\\cdot r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta \\\\  & ={∭}_{Δ}{r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\cdot r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}{\\int }_{0}^{8 \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}{\\int }_{1}^{2}r^{3}\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537058 :question/problem "If the solid region $R$ is given by \n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,(x-4)^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 16,y\\le 0,\\,y\\le z\\le 4\\},\n$$\n\nthen express the triple integral\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{z + 5}}\\,\\text{d}V\n$$\n\nas an equivalent integral in cylindrical polar coordinates." :question/worked-solution "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,dV={∭}_{Δ}\\,f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,z)\\,r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta .\n$$\n\nIf $(r,\\theta ,z)$ are the cylindrical polar coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the formulas\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,z=z.\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the projection of $R$ onto the $xy$-plane:\n\n![](images/51/510a5ecd48459be9372c1a797d00cc78a167b7ebc0563063e4f1a1e187e32bd0.png)\n\nThe region $R$ is one-half of a right vertical cylinder of radius $4$ whose axis passes through the point $(4,0)$ in the $xy$-plane, and is\n\n- bounded below by $z=y=r \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)},$ and\n\n- bounded above by $z=4.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write the $(x-4)^{2}+{y}^{2}=16$ in polar coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-4)^{2}+{y}^{2} & =16 \\\\ (r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta-4)^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta)^{2} & =16 \\\\ {r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -8r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta+16+{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta & =16 \\\\ {r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )-8r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta & =0 \\\\ {r}^{2}-8r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta & =0 \\\\ r(r-8\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $r$ cannot be identical to zero on the entire semicircle, we must have $r=8 \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}.$\n\nAs a result, in cylindrical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta ,z)\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 8 \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)},\\,\\frac{3 \\pi}{2}\\le \\theta \\le 2 \\pi,\\,r \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\le z\\le 4\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{z + 5}}\\,\\text{d}V & ={∭}_{Δ}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{z + 5}}\\cdot r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\frac{3 \\pi}{2}}^{2 \\pi}{\\int }_{0}^{8 \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}{\\int }_{r \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}^{4}\\frac{r}{\\sqrt{z + 5}}\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537064 :question/problem "If the solid region $R$ is given by \n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,(x-2)^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 4,y\\ge 0,\\,4\\le z\\le 6\\},\n$$\n\nthen express the triple integral\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}x^{2} + y^{2}\\,\\text{d}V\n$$\n\nas an equivalent integral in cylindrical polar coordinates." :question/worked-solution "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,dV={∭}_{Δ}\\,f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,z)\\,r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta .\n$$\n\nIf $(r,\\theta ,z)$ are the cylindrical polar coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the formulas\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,z=z.\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the projection of $R$ onto the $xy$-plane:\n\n![](images/d2/d2b3344b7b910f5685d4fd81b75a600608af6b008778ff608cb084edb5a92da9.png)\n\nThe region $R$ is one-half of a right vertical cylinder of radius $2$ whose axis passes through the point $(2,0)$ in the $xy$-plane, and is\n\n- bounded below by $z=4,$ and\n\n- bounded above by $z=6.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write the $(x-2)^{2}+{y}^{2}=4$ in polar coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-2)^{2}+{y}^{2} & =4 \\\\ (r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta-2)^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta)^{2} & =4 \\\\ {r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -4r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta+4+{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta & =4 \\\\ {r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )-4r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta & =0 \\\\ {r}^{2}-4r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta & =0 \\\\ r(r-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $r$ cannot be identical to zero on the entire semicircle, we must have $r=4 \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}.$\n\nAs a result, in cylindrical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta ,z)\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 4 \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)},\\,0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi}{2},\\,4\\le z\\le 6\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}x^{2} + y^{2}\\,\\text{d}V & ={∭}_{Δ}((r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta)^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta)^{2})\\cdot r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta \\\\  & ={∭}_{Δ}{r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\cdot r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}{\\int }_{0}^{4 \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}{\\int }_{4}^{6}r^{3}\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537066 :question/problem "If the solid region $R$ is given by \n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,(x-2)^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 4,y\\le 0,\\,y\\le z\\le 10\\},\n$$\n\nthen express the triple integral\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{z + 5}}\\,\\text{d}V\n$$\n\nas an equivalent integral in cylindrical polar coordinates." :question/worked-solution "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,dV={∭}_{Δ}\\,f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,z)\\,r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta .\n$$\n\nIf $(r,\\theta ,z)$ are the cylindrical polar coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the formulas\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,z=z.\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the projection of $R$ onto the $xy$-plane:\n\n![](images/d8/d8d5ba5f13ed464fa4e2b53fb2cd4fbdab92b3de371d3f7b535aeac8d0adb6d5.png)\n\nThe region $R$ is one-half of a right vertical cylinder of radius $2$ whose axis passes through the point $(2,0)$ in the $xy$-plane, and is\n\n- bounded below by $z=y=r \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)},$ and\n\n- bounded above by $z=10.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write the $(x-2)^{2}+{y}^{2}=4$ in polar coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-2)^{2}+{y}^{2} & =4 \\\\ (r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta-2)^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta)^{2} & =4 \\\\ {r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -4r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta+4+{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta & =4 \\\\ {r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )-4r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta & =0 \\\\ {r}^{2}-4r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta & =0 \\\\ r(r-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $r$ cannot be identical to zero on the entire semicircle, we must have $r=4 \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}.$\n\nAs a result, in cylindrical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta ,z)\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 4 \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)},\\,\\frac{3 \\pi}{2}\\le \\theta \\le 2 \\pi,\\,r \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\le z\\le 10\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{z + 5}}\\,\\text{d}V & ={∭}_{Δ}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{z + 5}}\\cdot r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\frac{3 \\pi}{2}}^{2 \\pi}{\\int }_{0}^{4 \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}{\\int }_{r \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}^{10}\\frac{r}{\\sqrt{z + 5}}\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537072 :question/problem "If the solid region $R$ is given by \n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+(y+4)^{2}\\le 16,\\,0\\le z\\le x^{2} + y^{2} + 2\\},\n$$\n\nthen express the triple integral\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}x y\\,\\text{d}V\n$$\n\nas an equivalent integral in cylindrical polar coordinates." :question/worked-solution "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,dV={∭}_{Δ}\\,f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,z)\\,r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta .\n$$\n\nIf $(r,\\theta ,z)$ are the cylindrical polar coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the formulas\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,z=z.\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the projection of $R$ onto the $xy$-plane:\n\n![](images/5e/5ed28b1e7b49276cdb59da432056597e5992b8c4015ac8e9384f6bfe3f1fb7ba.png)\n\nThe region $R$ is a right vertical cylinder of radius $4$ whose axis passes through the point $(0,-4)$ in the $xy$-plane, and is\n\n- bounded below by $z=0,$ and\n\n- bounded above by $z=x^{2} + y^{2} + 2=r^{2} + 2.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's write the equation ${x}^{2}+(y+4)^{2}=16$ in polar coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+(y+4)^{2} & =16 \\\\ (r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta)^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta+4)^{2} & =16 \\\\ {r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +8r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta+16 & =16 \\\\ {r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )+8r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta & =0 \\\\ {r}^{2}+8r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta & =0 \\\\ r(r+8\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $r$ cannot be identical to zero on the entire circle, we must have $r=- 8 \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}.$\n\nAs a result, in cylindrical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta ,z)\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le - 8 \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)},\\,\\pi\\le \\theta \\le 2 \\pi,\\,0\\le z\\le r^{2} + 2\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}x y\\,\\text{d}V & ={∭}_{Δ}(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta)(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta)\\cdot r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi}^{2 \\pi}{\\int }_{0}^{- 8 \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}{\\int }_{0}^{r^{2} + 2}r^{3} \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)} \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537084 :question/problem "Consider the plane curve $x^{2} + y^{2}=16.$  Find the equation of the normal line to the curve at the point $(-4,0).$" :question/worked-solution "If we set $f(x,y)=x^{2} + y^{2}$, our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=16.$  \n\nA normal vector to the level curve that passes through the point $(-4,0)$ can be found by computing $∇f(-4,0).$ \n\nSo, we find the gradient and evaluate it at $(-4,0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x^{2} + y^{2})\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x^{2} + y^{2})\\,j \\\\  & =(2 x)\\,i+(2 y)\\,j \\\\ ∇f(-4,0) & =(2 (-4))\\,i+(2 (0))\\,j \\\\  & =(-8)\\,i+(0)\\,j \\\\  & =⟨-8,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $∇f(-4,0)=⟨-8,0⟩$ is a normal vector to the curve at $(-4,0).$\n\nFinally, the Cartesian equation of the line that is parallel to $v=⟨{v}_{x},{v}_{y}⟩$ and passes through the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0})$ can be written as:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-{x}_{0}){v}_{y}-(y-{y}_{0}){v}_{x} & =0 \\\\ (x-(-4))(0)-(y-(0))(-8) & =0 \\\\ 8 y & =0 \\\\ y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537098 :question/problem "Consider the plane curve $3 x^{2} + 4 y^{2}=7.$  Find the equation of the normal line to the curve at the point $(1,-1).$" :question/worked-solution "If we set $f(x,y)=3 x^{2} + 4 y^{2}$, our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=7.$  \n\nA normal vector to the level curve that passes through the point $(1,-1)$ can be found by computing $∇f(1,-1).$ \n\nSo, we find the gradient and evaluate it at $(1,-1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(3 x^{2} + 4 y^{2})\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(3 x^{2} + 4 y^{2})\\,j \\\\  & =(6 x)\\,i+(8 y)\\,j \\\\ ∇f(1,-1) & =(6 (1))\\,i+(8 (-1))\\,j \\\\  & =(6)\\,i+(-8)\\,j \\\\  & =⟨6,-8⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $∇f(1,-1)=⟨6,-8⟩$ is a normal vector to the curve at $(1,-1).$\n\nFinally, the Cartesian equation of the line that is parallel to $v=⟨{v}_{x},{v}_{y}⟩$ and passes through the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0})$ can be written as:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x-{x}_{0}}{{v}_{x}} & =\\frac{y-{y}_{0}}{{v}_{y}} \\\\ \\frac{x-(1)}{6} & =\\frac{y-(-1)}{-8} \\\\ (-8)(x-(1)) & =(6)(y-(-1)) \\\\ (-4)(x-(1)) & =(3)(y-(-1)) \\\\ 4 - 4 x & =3 y + 3 \\\\ 4 x + 3 y - 1 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537099 :question/problem "Consider the plane curve $x^{2} + 2 y^{2}=9.$  Find the equation of the normal line to the curve at the point $(1,-2).$" :question/worked-solution "If we set $f(x,y)=x^{2} + 2 y^{2}$, our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=9.$  \n\nA normal vector to the level curve that passes through the point $(1,-2)$ can be found by computing $∇f(1,-2).$ \n\nSo, we find the gradient and evaluate it at $(1,-2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x^{2} + 2 y^{2})\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x^{2} + 2 y^{2})\\,j \\\\  & =(2 x)\\,i+(4 y)\\,j \\\\ ∇f(1,-2) & =(2 (1))\\,i+(4 (-2))\\,j \\\\  & =(2)\\,i+(-8)\\,j \\\\  & =⟨2,-8⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $∇f(1,-2)=⟨2,-8⟩$ is a normal vector to the curve at $(1,-2).$\n\nFinally, the Cartesian equation of the line that is parallel to $v=⟨{v}_{x},{v}_{y}⟩$ and passes through the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0})$ can be written as:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x-{x}_{0}}{{v}_{x}} & =\\frac{y-{y}_{0}}{{v}_{y}} \\\\ \\frac{x-(1)}{2} & =\\frac{y-(-2)}{-8} \\\\ (-8)(x-(1)) & =(2)(y-(-2)) \\\\ (-4)(x-(1)) & =(1)(y-(-2)) \\\\ 4 - 4 x & =y + 2 \\\\ 4 x + y - 2 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537100 :question/problem "Consider the plane curve $2 x^{2} + y^{2}=17.$  Find the equation of the normal line to the curve at the point $(-2,-3).$" :question/worked-solution "If we set $f(x,y)=2 x^{2} + y^{2}$, our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=17.$  \n\nA normal vector to the level curve that passes through the point $(-2,-3)$ can be found by computing $∇f(-2,-3).$ \n\nSo, we find the gradient and evaluate it at $(-2,-3):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2 x^{2} + y^{2})\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2 x^{2} + y^{2})\\,j \\\\  & =(4 x)\\,i+(2 y)\\,j \\\\ ∇f(-2,-3) & =(4 (-2))\\,i+(2 (-3))\\,j \\\\  & =(-8)\\,i+(-6)\\,j \\\\  & =⟨-8,-6⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $∇f(-2,-3)=⟨-8,-6⟩$ is a normal vector to the curve at $(-2,-3).$\n\nFinally, the Cartesian equation of the line that is parallel to $v=⟨{v}_{x},{v}_{y}⟩$ and passes through the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0})$ can be written as:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x-{x}_{0}}{{v}_{x}} & =\\frac{y-{y}_{0}}{{v}_{y}} \\\\ \\frac{x-(-2)}{-8} & =\\frac{y-(-3)}{-6} \\\\ (-6)(x-(-2)) & =(-8)(y-(-3)) \\\\ (-3)(x-(-2)) & =(-4)(y-(-3)) \\\\ - 3 x - 6 & =- 4 y - 12 \\\\ 3 x - 4 y - 6 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537113 :question/problem "Consider the plane curve $3 x^{2} + y^{2}=36.$  Find the equation of the normal line to the curve at the point $(3,-3).$" :question/worked-solution "If we set $f(x,y)=3 x^{2} + y^{2}$, our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=36.$  \n\nA normal vector to the level curve that passes through the point $(3,-3)$ can be found by computing $∇f(3,-3).$ \n\nSo, we find the gradient and evaluate it at $(3,-3):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(3 x^{2} + y^{2})\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(3 x^{2} + y^{2})\\,j \\\\  & =(6 x)\\,i+(2 y)\\,j \\\\ ∇f(3,-3) & =(6 (3))\\,i+(2 (-3))\\,j \\\\  & =(18)\\,i+(-6)\\,j \\\\  & =⟨18,-6⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $∇f(3,-3)=⟨18,-6⟩$ is a normal vector to the curve at $(3,-3).$\n\nFinally, the Cartesian equation of the line that is parallel to $v=⟨{v}_{x},{v}_{y}⟩$ and passes through the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0})$ can be written as:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x-{x}_{0}}{{v}_{x}} & =\\frac{y-{y}_{0}}{{v}_{y}} \\\\ \\frac{x-(3)}{18} & =\\frac{y-(-3)}{-6} \\\\ (-6)(x-(3)) & =(18)(y-(-3)) \\\\ (-1)(x-(3)) & =(3)(y-(-3)) \\\\ 3 - x & =3 y + 9 \\\\ x + 3 y + 6 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537122 :question/problem "Consider the plane curve $4 x^{2} + 2 y^{2}=22.$  Find the equation of the normal line to the curve at the point $(-1,3).$" :question/worked-solution "If we set $f(x,y)=4 x^{2} + 2 y^{2}$, our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=22.$  \n\nA normal vector to the level curve that passes through the point $(-1,3)$ can be found by computing $∇f(-1,3).$ \n\nSo, we find the gradient and evaluate it at $(-1,3):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(4 x^{2} + 2 y^{2})\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(4 x^{2} + 2 y^{2})\\,j \\\\  & =(8 x)\\,i+(4 y)\\,j \\\\ ∇f(-1,3) & =(8 (-1))\\,i+(4 (3))\\,j \\\\  & =(-8)\\,i+(12)\\,j \\\\  & =⟨-8,12⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $∇f(-1,3)=⟨-8,12⟩$ is a normal vector to the curve at $(-1,3).$\n\nFinally, the Cartesian equation of the line that is parallel to $v=⟨{v}_{x},{v}_{y}⟩$ and passes through the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0})$ can be written as:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x-{x}_{0}}{{v}_{x}} & =\\frac{y-{y}_{0}}{{v}_{y}} \\\\ \\frac{x-(-1)}{-8} & =\\frac{y-(3)}{12} \\\\ (12)(x-(-1)) & =(-8)(y-(3)) \\\\ (3)(x-(-1)) & =(-2)(y-(3)) \\\\ 3 x + 3 & =6 - 2 y \\\\ 3 x + 2 y - 3 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537130 :question/problem "Consider the plane curve $\\frac{x^{2}}{9} + y^{2}=1.$  Find the equation of the normal line to the curve at the point $(-3,0).$" :question/worked-solution "If we set $f(x,y)=\\frac{x^{2}}{9} + y^{2}$, our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=1.$  \n\nA normal vector to the level curve that passes through the point $(-3,0)$ can be found by computing $∇f(-3,0).$ \n\nSo, we find the gradient and evaluate it at $(-3,0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{x^{2}}{9} + y^{2})\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\frac{x^{2}}{9} + y^{2})\\,j \\\\  & =\\left(\\frac{2 x}{9}\\right)\\,i+(2 y)\\,j \\\\ ∇f(-3,0) & =\\left(\\frac{2 (-3)}{9}\\right)\\,i+(2 (0))\\,j \\\\  & =\\left(- \\frac{2}{3}\\right)\\,i+(0)\\,j \\\\  & =⟨- \\frac{2}{3},0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $∇f(-3,0)=⟨- \\frac{2}{3},0⟩$ is a normal vector to the curve at $(-3,0).$\n\nFinally, the Cartesian equation of the line that is parallel to $v=⟨{v}_{x},{v}_{y}⟩$ and passes through the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0})$ can be written as:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-{x}_{0}){v}_{y}-(y-{y}_{0}){v}_{x} & =0 \\\\ (x-(-3))(0)-(y-(0))\\left(- \\frac{2}{3}\\right) & =0 \\\\ \\frac{2 y}{3} & =0 \\\\ y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537903 :question/problem "Given that \n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}{y}^{3}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}x-{x}^{3}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}y={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\hspace{-0.167em}{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\enclose{box}{\\phantom{AAAAAAAA}}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}r\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}\\theta ,\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is the circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=4$ oriented counterclockwise, what is the missing part of the equation?" :question/worked-solution "Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn our case, the region $D$ and its boundary $C$ satisfy the conditions of Green's theorem:\n\n![](images/79/798665574b8f0e11d991bb0d934efd01bdeb65840c2e86498be4edbc59578439.png)\n\nSince $P(x,y)={y}^{3}$ and $Q(x,y)=-{x}^{3},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=3{y}^{2},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=-3{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}{y}^{3}\\,dx-{x}^{3}\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-3{x}^{2}-3{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the region $D$ can be represented in polar coordinates as\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 2,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nThe change of variables formula for plane polar coordinates states that \n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our double integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}(-3{x}^{2}-3{y}^{2})\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{\\mathrm{Δ}}(-3\\cdot (r\\operatorname{cos}\\theta )^{2}-3\\cdot (r\\operatorname{sin}\\theta )^{2})\\hspace{0.222em}r\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={∬}_{\\mathrm{Δ}}(-3{r}^{2}{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta -3{r}^{2}{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta )\\hspace{0.222em}r\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={∬}_{\\mathrm{Δ}}-3{r}^{3}({\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta +{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta )\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\hspace{-0.167em}{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{-3{r}^{3}}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}r\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537906 :question/problem "Consider the triangle $C$ in the plane with vertices $(0,0),(1,1)$ and $(0,1),$ and the region $D$ bounded by $C.$ According to Green's theorem, \n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\,dx+(xy+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y)\\,dy={∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nCalculate the function $f(x,y).$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D$, then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](images/56/5673a3eadfa3c01dbb9e2c8bf497b17f34a834d950621afa7d480af625ce1e18.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y$ and $Q(x,y)=xy+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=y-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\,dx+(xy+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y)\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}[y-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y-(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y)]\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(y-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}y\\,\\text{d}A\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f(x,y)=y.$"}]}
